DirectWrite lists a right-to-left run's glyphs in reading order and advances them leftwards from the run's right edge; place them left to right and return them in visual order as CoreText does. Lines that open with a right-to-left letter get a right-to-left reading direction (with a finite width and no wrapping, so run origins stay in the line), so neutral characters and embedded Latin words sit where macOS puts them. Laid left to right in reading order, Arabic letters were reversed and their joining forms fell apart.

